{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-138944" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082905/state.json" "file_sha256" "0c52eab5f19a56af72dfd73c0a6c2de6c87bfe905a74bd6bf942caa8d15febf8"} {"question" "q-138944" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡2t+{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the identity $\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,$ we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡2t+{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{4(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{16{sin}^{2}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t(16{cos}^{2}⁡t+1)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot \\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\cdot \\sqrt{16{cos}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot |\\operatorname{sin}⁡t|\\cdot \\sqrt{16{cos}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{16{cos}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082905/state.json" "file_sha256" "0c52eab5f19a56af72dfd73c0a6c2de6c87bfe905a74bd6bf942caa8d15febf8"} {"question" "q-138944" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082905/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9e495b45b9f9f340fc5fc19e8d1eba79960436827d96258142552a13345afc16"} {"question" "q-138944" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082905/state.json" "file_sha256" "0c52eab5f19a56af72dfd73c0a6c2de6c87bfe905a74bd6bf942caa8d15febf8"} {"question" "q-138944" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{16{cos}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{sin}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{9{cos}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{cos}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{8{cos}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{cos}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{16{cos}^{2}⁡t-1}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{cos}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡t-1}\\,\\text{d}t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082905/state.json" "file_sha256" "0c52eab5f19a56af72dfd73c0a6c2de6c87bfe905a74bd6bf942caa8d15febf8"} {"question" "q-138944" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{16{cos}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082905/state.json" "file_sha256" "0c52eab5f19a56af72dfd73c0a6c2de6c87bfe905a74bd6bf942caa8d15febf8"} {"question" "q-139063" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y={e}^{t},\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082905/state.json" "file_sha256" "0c52eab5f19a56af72dfd73c0a6c2de6c87bfe905a74bd6bf942caa8d15febf8"} {"question" "q-139063" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=1.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})={e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{({e}^{t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t+{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082905/state.json" "file_sha256" "0c52eab5f19a56af72dfd73c0a6c2de6c87bfe905a74bd6bf942caa8d15febf8"} {"question" "q-139063" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082905/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9e495b45b9f9f340fc5fc19e8d1eba79960436827d96258142552a13345afc16"} {"question" "q-139063" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082905/state.json" "file_sha256" "0c52eab5f19a56af72dfd73c0a6c2de6c87bfe905a74bd6bf942caa8d15febf8"} {"question" "q-139063" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡t+{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{e}^{t})\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t+{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}t\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{e}^{t}}\\,\\text{d}t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082905/state.json" "file_sha256" "0c52eab5f19a56af72dfd73c0a6c2de6c87bfe905a74bd6bf942caa8d15febf8"} {"question" "q-139063" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t+{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082905/state.json" "file_sha256" "0c52eab5f19a56af72dfd73c0a6c2de6c87bfe905a74bd6bf942caa8d15febf8"} {"question" "q-139067" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=5t+1,\\,y={t}^{2},\\,t\\in [0,3].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082905/state.json" "file_sha256" "0c52eab5f19a56af72dfd73c0a6c2de6c87bfe905a74bd6bf942caa8d15febf8"} {"question" "q-139067" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=3.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5t+1)=5 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})=2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{{(5)}^{2}+{(2t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{25+4{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082905/state.json" "file_sha256" "0c52eab5f19a56af72dfd73c0a6c2de6c87bfe905a74bd6bf942caa8d15febf8"} {"question" "q-139067" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082905/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9e495b45b9f9f340fc5fc19e8d1eba79960436827d96258142552a13345afc16"} {"question" "q-139067" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082905/state.json" "file_sha256" "0c52eab5f19a56af72dfd73c0a6c2de6c87bfe905a74bd6bf942caa8d15febf8"} {"question" "q-139067" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "10\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}t\\sqrt{5+2{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "10\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}(5t+1)^{2}\\sqrt{5+2t}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{25+4{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "5\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}{t}^{2}(5t+1)\\,\\text{d}t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082905/state.json" "file_sha256" "0c52eab5f19a56af72dfd73c0a6c2de6c87bfe905a74bd6bf942caa8d15febf8"} {"question" "q-139067" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{25+4{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082905/state.json" "file_sha256" "0c52eab5f19a56af72dfd73c0a6c2de6c87bfe905a74bd6bf942caa8d15febf8"} {"question" "q-139069" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx={e}^{t},\\,y=4t,\\,t\\in [0,2].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082905/state.json" "file_sha256" "0c52eab5f19a56af72dfd73c0a6c2de6c87bfe905a74bd6bf942caa8d15febf8"} {"question" "q-139069" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=2.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})={e}^{t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t)=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}4t\\sqrt{{({e}^{t})}^{2}+{(4)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}t\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+16}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082905/state.json" "file_sha256" "0c52eab5f19a56af72dfd73c0a6c2de6c87bfe905a74bd6bf942caa8d15febf8"} {"question" "q-139069" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082905/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9e495b45b9f9f340fc5fc19e8d1eba79960436827d96258142552a13345afc16"} {"question" "q-139069" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082905/state.json" "file_sha256" "0c52eab5f19a56af72dfd73c0a6c2de6c87bfe905a74bd6bf942caa8d15febf8"} {"question" "q-139069" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "16\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}t{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{{e}^{t}+4}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "8\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}t\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+16}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "8\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}t\\sqrt{{e}^{t}+16{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "8\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082905/state.json" "file_sha256" "0c52eab5f19a56af72dfd73c0a6c2de6c87bfe905a74bd6bf942caa8d15febf8"} {"question" "q-139069" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "8\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}t\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+16}\\,\\text{d}t"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082905/state.json" "file_sha256" "0c52eab5f19a56af72dfd73c0a6c2de6c87bfe905a74bd6bf942caa8d15febf8"} {"question" "q-138968" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx={t}^{2}+1,\\,y={t}^{3},\\,t\\in [0,2].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082905/state.json" "file_sha256" "0c52eab5f19a56af72dfd73c0a6c2de6c87bfe905a74bd6bf942caa8d15febf8"} {"question" "q-138968" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=2.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+1)=2t \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})=3{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{3}\\sqrt{{(2t)}^{2}+{(3{t}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{3}\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+9{t}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{3}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}(4+9{t}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{3}\\cdot \\sqrt{{t}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{(4+9{t}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{3}\\cdot |t|\\cdot \\sqrt{9{t}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{3}\\cdot t\\cdot \\sqrt{9{t}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{4}\\sqrt{9{t}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082905/state.json" "file_sha256" "0c52eab5f19a56af72dfd73c0a6c2de6c87bfe905a74bd6bf942caa8d15febf8"} {"question" "q-138968" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082905/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9e495b45b9f9f340fc5fc19e8d1eba79960436827d96258142552a13345afc16"} {"question" "q-138968" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082905/state.json" "file_sha256" "0c52eab5f19a56af72dfd73c0a6c2de6c87bfe905a74bd6bf942caa8d15febf8"} {"question" "q-138968" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}({t}^{2}+1)\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+9t}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{3}\\sqrt{3{t}^{4}+2}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{4}\\sqrt{9{t}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{3}\\sqrt{3{t}^{3}+2t}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{4}\\sqrt{{t}^{3}+{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082905/state.json" "file_sha256" "0c52eab5f19a56af72dfd73c0a6c2de6c87bfe905a74bd6bf942caa8d15febf8"} {"question" "q-138968" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{4}\\sqrt{9{t}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}t"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082905/state.json" "file_sha256" "0c52eab5f19a56af72dfd73c0a6c2de6c87bfe905a74bd6bf942caa8d15febf8"} {"question" "q-139027" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2t,\\,t\\in [0,2].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082905/state.json" "file_sha256" "0c52eab5f19a56af72dfd73c0a6c2de6c87bfe905a74bd6bf942caa8d15febf8"} {"question" "q-139027" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=2.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡t)=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2t)=2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡t\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{sinh}⁡t)}^{2}+{(2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡t\\sqrt{{sinh}^{2}⁡t+4{sinh}^{2}⁡2t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the identity $\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2t=2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡t\\operatorname{cosh}⁡t,$ we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡t\\sqrt{{sinh}^{2}⁡t+4{sinh}^{2}⁡2t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡t\\sqrt{{sinh}^{2}⁡t+4(2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡t\\operatorname{cosh}⁡t)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡t\\sqrt{{sinh}^{2}⁡t+16{sinh}^{2}⁡t{cosh}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡t\\sqrt{{sinh}^{2}⁡t(1+16{cosh}^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡t\\cdot \\sqrt{{sinh}^{2}⁡t}\\cdot \\sqrt{1+16{cosh}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡t\\cdot |\\operatorname{sinh}⁡t|\\cdot \\sqrt{1+16{cosh}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡t\\operatorname{cosh}⁡t\\sqrt{1+16{cosh}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082905/state.json" "file_sha256" "0c52eab5f19a56af72dfd73c0a6c2de6c87bfe905a74bd6bf942caa8d15febf8"} {"question" "q-139027" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082905/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9e495b45b9f9f340fc5fc19e8d1eba79960436827d96258142552a13345afc16"} {"question" "q-139027" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082905/state.json" "file_sha256" "0c52eab5f19a56af72dfd73c0a6c2de6c87bfe905a74bd6bf942caa8d15febf8"} {"question" "q-139027" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡t\\operatorname{cosh}⁡t\\sqrt{1+16{cosh}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{sinh}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{1+8{cosh}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "8\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡t\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2t\\sqrt{1+4{cosh}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "8\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡t{cosh}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082905/state.json" "file_sha256" "0c52eab5f19a56af72dfd73c0a6c2de6c87bfe905a74bd6bf942caa8d15febf8"} {"question" "q-139027" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡t\\operatorname{cosh}⁡t\\sqrt{1+16{cosh}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082905/state.json" "file_sha256" "0c52eab5f19a56af72dfd73c0a6c2de6c87bfe905a74bd6bf942caa8d15febf8"} {"question" "q-138981" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,t\\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082905/state.json" "file_sha256" "0c52eab5f19a56af72dfd73c0a6c2de6c87bfe905a74bd6bf942caa8d15febf8"} {"question" "q-138981" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=\\pi .$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{4{cos}^{2}⁡t+4{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{4({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot 2\\cdot \\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the identity ${sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1,$ we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =8\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082905/state.json" "file_sha256" "0c52eab5f19a56af72dfd73c0a6c2de6c87bfe905a74bd6bf942caa8d15febf8"} {"question" "q-138981" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082905/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9e495b45b9f9f340fc5fc19e8d1eba79960436827d96258142552a13345afc16"} {"question" "q-138981" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082905/state.json" "file_sha256" "0c52eab5f19a56af72dfd73c0a6c2de6c87bfe905a74bd6bf942caa8d15febf8"} {"question" "q-138981" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\sqrt{2}\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "2\\sqrt{2}\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "8\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "8\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082905/state.json" "file_sha256" "0c52eab5f19a56af72dfd73c0a6c2de6c87bfe905a74bd6bf942caa8d15febf8"} {"question" "q-138981" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "8\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082905/state.json" "file_sha256" "0c52eab5f19a56af72dfd73c0a6c2de6c87bfe905a74bd6bf942caa8d15febf8"} {"question" "q-139056" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx={t}^{3},\\,y=2t,\\,t\\in [0,2].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082905/state.json" "file_sha256" "0c52eab5f19a56af72dfd73c0a6c2de6c87bfe905a74bd6bf942caa8d15febf8"} {"question" "q-139056" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=2.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})=3{t}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{3}\\sqrt{{(3{t}^{2})}^{2}+{(2)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{3}\\sqrt{9{t}^{4}+4}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082905/state.json" "file_sha256" "0c52eab5f19a56af72dfd73c0a6c2de6c87bfe905a74bd6bf942caa8d15febf8"} {"question" "q-139056" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082905/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9e495b45b9f9f340fc5fc19e8d1eba79960436827d96258142552a13345afc16"} {"question" "q-139056" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082905/state.json" "file_sha256" "0c52eab5f19a56af72dfd73c0a6c2de6c87bfe905a74bd6bf942caa8d15febf8"} {"question" "q-139056" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}t\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+2}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{3}\\sqrt{9{t}^{4}+4}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}t\\sqrt{3{t}^{4}+2}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{3}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "6\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{5}\\,\\text{d}t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082905/state.json" "file_sha256" "0c52eab5f19a56af72dfd73c0a6c2de6c87bfe905a74bd6bf942caa8d15febf8"} {"question" "q-139056" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{3}\\sqrt{9{t}^{4}+4}\\,\\text{d}t"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082905/state.json" "file_sha256" "0c52eab5f19a56af72dfd73c0a6c2de6c87bfe905a74bd6bf942caa8d15febf8"} {"question" "q-139033" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx={cos}^{2}⁡t,\\,y={sin}^{2}⁡t,\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082905/state.json" "file_sha256" "0c52eab5f19a56af72dfd73c0a6c2de6c87bfe905a74bd6bf942caa8d15febf8"} {"question" "q-139033" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({cos}^{2}⁡t)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({sin}^{2}⁡t)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t+4{sin}^{2}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{8{sin}^{2}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡t\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2}\\cdot |\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082905/state.json" "file_sha256" "0c52eab5f19a56af72dfd73c0a6c2de6c87bfe905a74bd6bf942caa8d15febf8"} {"question" "q-139033" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082905/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9e495b45b9f9f340fc5fc19e8d1eba79960436827d96258142552a13345afc16"} {"question" "q-139033" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082905/state.json" "file_sha256" "0c52eab5f19a56af72dfd73c0a6c2de6c87bfe905a74bd6bf942caa8d15febf8"} {"question" "q-139033" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "2\\sqrt{2}\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "4\\sqrt{2}\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "8\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082905/state.json" "file_sha256" "0c52eab5f19a56af72dfd73c0a6c2de6c87bfe905a74bd6bf942caa8d15febf8"} {"question" "q-139033" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4\\sqrt{2}\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082905/state.json" "file_sha256" "0c52eab5f19a56af72dfd73c0a6c2de6c87bfe905a74bd6bf942caa8d15febf8"} {"question" "q-139058" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx={3}^{t},\\,y={e}^{t},\\,t\\in [0,3].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082905/state.json" "file_sha256" "0c52eab5f19a56af72dfd73c0a6c2de6c87bfe905a74bd6bf942caa8d15febf8"} {"question" "q-139058" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=3.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({3}^{t})={3}^{t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})={e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}{3}^{t}\\sqrt{{({3}^{t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)}^{2}+{({e}^{t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}{3}^{t}\\sqrt{{3}^{2t}{ln}^{2}⁡3+{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}{3}^{t}\\sqrt{{9}^{t}{ln}^{2}⁡3+{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082905/state.json" "file_sha256" "0c52eab5f19a56af72dfd73c0a6c2de6c87bfe905a74bd6bf942caa8d15febf8"} {"question" "q-139058" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082905/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9e495b45b9f9f340fc5fc19e8d1eba79960436827d96258142552a13345afc16"} {"question" "q-139058" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082905/state.json" "file_sha256" "0c52eab5f19a56af72dfd73c0a6c2de6c87bfe905a74bd6bf942caa8d15febf8"} {"question" "q-139058" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\pi \\operatorname{ln}⁡3{\\int }_{0}^{3}{3}^{2t}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}{3}^{t}\\sqrt{{9}^{t}{ln}^{2}⁡3+{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{{3}^{t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+{e}^{t}}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "6\\pi \\operatorname{ln}⁡3{\\int }_{0}^{3}{3}^{t}{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["math" "6\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}{3}^{2t}\\sqrt{{3}^{t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+{e}^{t}}\\,\\text{d}t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082905/state.json" "file_sha256" "0c52eab5f19a56af72dfd73c0a6c2de6c87bfe905a74bd6bf942caa8d15febf8"} {"question" "q-139058" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}{3}^{t}\\sqrt{{9}^{t}{ln}^{2}⁡3+{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082905/state.json" "file_sha256" "0c52eab5f19a56af72dfd73c0a6c2de6c87bfe905a74bd6bf942caa8d15febf8"} {"question" "q-352089" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx={e}^{t}-t,\\,y=4{e}^{t/2},\\,t\\in [0,\\operatorname{ln}⁡4].\n$$\n\nDetermine the surface area $S$ generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$S=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082905/state.json" "file_sha256" "0c52eab5f19a56af72dfd73c0a6c2de6c87bfe905a74bd6bf942caa8d15febf8"} {"question" "q-352089" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡4.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}-t)={e}^{t}-1, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{e}^{t/2})=2{e}^{t/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}4{e}^{t/2}\\sqrt{{({e}^{t}-1)}^{2}+{(2{e}^{t/2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}4{e}^{t/2}\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}-2{e}^{t}+1+4{e}^{t}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}4{e}^{t/2}\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+2{e}^{t}+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}4{e}^{t/2}\\sqrt{({e}^{t}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}4{e}^{t/2}({e}^{t}+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}8\\pi {e}^{t/2}({e}^{t}+1)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the last integral using the substitution $u={e}^{t/2}.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{t/2}\\,\\text{d}t,$ or $2\\,\\text{d}u={e}^{t/2}\\,\\text{d}t.$ We also update the limits of integration for $u:$\n\n| **$t$** | $0$ | $\\operatorname{ln}⁡4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}8\\pi ({e}^{t}+1)\\cdot {e}^{t/2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}8\\pi ({u}^{2}+1)\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =16\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}({u}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =16\\pi {[\\frac{{u}^{3}}{3}+u]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =16\\pi ((\\frac{8}{3}+2)-(\\frac{1}{3}+1)) \\\\  & =16\\pi (\\frac{14}{3}-\\frac{4}{3}) \\\\  & =16\\pi (\\frac{10}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{160\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082905/state.json" "file_sha256" "0c52eab5f19a56af72dfd73c0a6c2de6c87bfe905a74bd6bf942caa8d15febf8"} {"question" "q-352089" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082905/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9e495b45b9f9f340fc5fc19e8d1eba79960436827d96258142552a13345afc16"} {"question" "q-352089" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082905/state.json" "file_sha256" "0c52eab5f19a56af72dfd73c0a6c2de6c87bfe905a74bd6bf942caa8d15febf8"} {"question" "q-352089" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{160\\pi}{3}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082905/state.json" "file_sha256" "0c52eab5f19a56af72dfd73c0a6c2de6c87bfe905a74bd6bf942caa8d15febf8"} {"question" "q-138979" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx={e}^{t}-t,\\,y=4{e}^{t/2},\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nCalculate the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082905/state.json" "file_sha256" "0c52eab5f19a56af72dfd73c0a6c2de6c87bfe905a74bd6bf942caa8d15febf8"} {"question" "q-138979" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=1.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}-t)={e}^{t}-1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{e}^{t/2})=2{e}^{t/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{t}-t)\\sqrt{{({e}^{t}-1)}^{2}+{(2{e}^{t/2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{t}-t)\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}-2{e}^{t}+1+4{e}^{t}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{t}-t)\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+2{e}^{t}+1}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, factoring the expression ${e}^{2t}+2{e}^{t}+1,$ we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{t}-t)\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+2{e}^{t}+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{t}-t)\\sqrt{({e}^{t}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{t}-t)\\cdot |{e}^{t}+1|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{t}-t)\\cdot ({e}^{t}+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{2t}+{e}^{t}-t{e}^{t}-t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi [{\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{2t}+{e}^{t}-t)\\,\\text{d}t-{\\int }_{0}^{1}t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{1}t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t$ using integration by parts. We set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=t & ⇒ & {u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{t} & ⇒ & v={e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}x & =t{e}^{t}-\\int {e}^{t}\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =t{e}^{t}-{e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi [{\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{2t}+{e}^{t}-t)\\,\\text{d}t-{\\int }_{0}^{1}t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t] \\\\  & =2\\pi [{(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}+{e}^{t}-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2})|}_{0}^{1}-{(t{e}^{t}-{e}^{t})|}_{0}^{1}] \\\\  & =2\\pi [(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2}+e-\\frac{1}{2})-(\\frac{1}{2}+1-0)-(e-e)+(0-1)] \\\\  & =2\\pi [\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2}+e-3] \\\\  & =({e}^{2}+2e-6)\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082905/state.json" "file_sha256" "0c52eab5f19a56af72dfd73c0a6c2de6c87bfe905a74bd6bf942caa8d15febf8"} {"question" "q-138979" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082905/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9e495b45b9f9f340fc5fc19e8d1eba79960436827d96258142552a13345afc16"} {"question" "q-138979" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082905/state.json" "file_sha256" "0c52eab5f19a56af72dfd73c0a6c2de6c87bfe905a74bd6bf942caa8d15febf8"} {"question" "q-138979" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(2{e}^{2}-e+3)\\pi "] ["math" "({e}^{2}+2e-6)\\pi "] ["math" "({e}^{2}+3e-1)\\pi "] ["math" "({e}^{2}+6e)\\pi "] ["math" "({e}^{2}-3)\\pi "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082905/state.json" "file_sha256" "0c52eab5f19a56af72dfd73c0a6c2de6c87bfe905a74bd6bf942caa8d15febf8"} {"question" "q-138979" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "({e}^{2}+2e-6)\\pi "] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082905/state.json" "file_sha256" "0c52eab5f19a56af72dfd73c0a6c2de6c87bfe905a74bd6bf942caa8d15febf8"} {"question" "q-139013" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=3{t}^{2},\\,y=2{t}^{3},\\,t\\in [0,\\sqrt{3}].\n$$\n\nCalculate the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082905/state.json" "file_sha256" "0c52eab5f19a56af72dfd73c0a6c2de6c87bfe905a74bd6bf942caa8d15febf8"} {"question" "q-139013" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=\\sqrt{3}.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2})=6t \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3})=6{t}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}3{t}^{2}\\sqrt{{(6t)}^{2}+{(6{t}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}3{t}^{2}\\sqrt{36{t}^{2}+36{t}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, factoring the expression $36{t}^{2}+36{t}^{4},$ we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}3{t}^{2}\\sqrt{36{t}^{2}+36{t}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}t] \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}3{t}^{2}\\sqrt{36{t}^{2}(1+{t}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}3{t}^{2}\\cdot |6t|\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}3{t}^{2}\\cdot 6t\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =18\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}2{t}^{3}\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the last integral using the substitution $u=1+{t}^{2}$ Then, ${t}^{2}=u-1$ and $\\text{d}u=2t\\,\\text{d}t.$ We also need to compute new limits, given below.\n\n| **$t$** | $0$ | $\\sqrt{3}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $4$ |\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =18\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}2{t}^{3}\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =18\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}2t\\cdot {t}^{2}\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =18\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{4}(u-1)\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =18\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{4}(u-1){u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =18\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{4}({u}^{3/2}-{u}^{1/2})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =18\\pi \\cdot {[\\frac{2{u}^{5/2}}{5}-\\frac{2{u}^{3/2}}{3}]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =18\\pi [(\\frac{2({4}^{5/2})}{5}-\\frac{2({4}^{3/2})}{3})-(\\frac{2}{5}-\\frac{2}{3})] \\\\  & =18\\pi \\cdot [(\\frac{64}{5}-\\frac{16}{3})-(\\frac{2}{5}-\\frac{2}{3})) \\\\  & =18\\pi \\cdot (\\frac{62}{5}-\\frac{14}{3}) \\\\  & =18\\pi \\cdot \\frac{116}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{696\\pi }{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082905/state.json" "file_sha256" "0c52eab5f19a56af72dfd73c0a6c2de6c87bfe905a74bd6bf942caa8d15febf8"} {"question" "q-139013" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082905/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9e495b45b9f9f340fc5fc19e8d1eba79960436827d96258142552a13345afc16"} {"question" "q-139013" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082905/state.json" "file_sha256" "0c52eab5f19a56af72dfd73c0a6c2de6c87bfe905a74bd6bf942caa8d15febf8"} {"question" "q-139013" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{112\\pi }{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{112\\sqrt{6}\\pi }{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{142\\pi }{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{696\\pi }{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{9\\sqrt{10}\\pi }{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082905/state.json" "file_sha256" "0c52eab5f19a56af72dfd73c0a6c2de6c87bfe905a74bd6bf942caa8d15febf8"} {"question" "q-139013" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{696\\pi }{5}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082905/state.json" "file_sha256" "0c52eab5f19a56af72dfd73c0a6c2de6c87bfe905a74bd6bf942caa8d15febf8"} {"question" "q-348298" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=6t-2{t}^{3},\\,y=6{t}^{2},\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nDetermine the surface area $S$ generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$S=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082905/state.json" "file_sha256" "0c52eab5f19a56af72dfd73c0a6c2de6c87bfe905a74bd6bf942caa8d15febf8"} {"question" "q-348298" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=1.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6t-2{t}^{3})=6-6{t}^{2}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6{t}^{2})=12t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}6{t}^{2}\\sqrt{{(6-6{t}^{2})}^{2}+{(12t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}6{t}^{2}\\sqrt{36-72{t}^{2}+36{t}^{4}+144{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}6{t}^{2}\\sqrt{36{t}^{4}+72{t}^{2}+36}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, factoring the expression $36{t}^{4}+72{t}^{2}+36,$ we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}6{t}^{2}\\sqrt{36({t}^{4}+2{t}^{2}+1)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}6{t}^{2}\\cdot 6\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+2{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =72\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{({t}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =72\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}\\cdot |{t}^{2}+1|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =72\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}({t}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =72\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{4}+{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =72\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{4}+{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =72\\pi \\cdot {[\\frac{{t}^{5}}{5}+\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =72\\pi [(\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{1}{3})-(\\frac{0}{5}+\\frac{0}{3})] \\\\  & =72\\pi \\cdot \\frac{8}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{192\\pi }{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082905/state.json" "file_sha256" "0c52eab5f19a56af72dfd73c0a6c2de6c87bfe905a74bd6bf942caa8d15febf8"} {"question" "q-348298" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082905/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9e495b45b9f9f340fc5fc19e8d1eba79960436827d96258142552a13345afc16"} {"question" "q-348298" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082905/state.json" "file_sha256" "0c52eab5f19a56af72dfd73c0a6c2de6c87bfe905a74bd6bf942caa8d15febf8"} {"question" "q-348298" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{192\\pi}{5}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082905/state.json" "file_sha256" "0c52eab5f19a56af72dfd73c0a6c2de6c87bfe905a74bd6bf942caa8d15febf8"} {"question" "q-139025" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,\\,y=t+\\frac{1}{t},\\,t\\in [1,e].\n$$\n\nCalculate the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082905/state.json" "file_sha256" "0c52eab5f19a56af72dfd73c0a6c2de6c87bfe905a74bd6bf942caa8d15febf8"} {"question" "q-139025" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=1$ to ${t}_{2}=e.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)=\\frac{2}{t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+\\frac{1}{t})=1-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}(t+\\frac{1}{t})\\sqrt{{(\\frac{2}{t})}^{2}+{(1-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}(t+\\frac{1}{t})\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{{t}^{2}}+1-\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}(t+\\frac{1}{t})\\sqrt{1+\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}}}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, factoring the expression $1+\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}},$ we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}(t+\\frac{1}{t})\\sqrt{1+\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}(t+\\frac{1}{t})\\sqrt{{(1+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}})}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}(t+\\frac{1}{t})\\cdot |1+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}(t+\\frac{1}{t})\\cdot (1+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}(\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}}+t+\\frac{2}{t})\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}(\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}}+t+\\frac{2}{t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi \\cdot {[-\\frac{1}{2{t}^{2}}+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|]}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =2\\pi [(-\\frac{1}{2{e}^{2}}+\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2}+2)-(-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}+0)] \\\\  & =2\\pi [\\frac{{e}^{4}-1}{2{e}^{2}}+2] \\\\  & =(\\frac{{e}^{4}-1}{{e}^{2}}+4)\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082905/state.json" "file_sha256" "0c52eab5f19a56af72dfd73c0a6c2de6c87bfe905a74bd6bf942caa8d15febf8"} {"question" "q-139025" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082905/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9e495b45b9f9f340fc5fc19e8d1eba79960436827d96258142552a13345afc16"} {"question" "q-139025" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082905/state.json" "file_sha256" "0c52eab5f19a56af72dfd73c0a6c2de6c87bfe905a74bd6bf942caa8d15febf8"} {"question" "q-139025" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(\\frac{{e}^{2}+1}{{e}^{2}}+1)\\pi "] ["math" "(\\frac{{e}^{2}+2}{{e}^{2}})\\pi "] ["math" "(\\frac{{e}^{4}+1}{{e}^{2}}+2)\\pi "] ["math" "(\\frac{{e}^{4}-1}{{e}^{2}})\\pi "] ["math" "(\\frac{{e}^{4}-1}{{e}^{2}}+4)\\pi "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082905/state.json" "file_sha256" "0c52eab5f19a56af72dfd73c0a6c2de6c87bfe905a74bd6bf942caa8d15febf8"} {"question" "q-139025" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(\\frac{{e}^{4}-1}{{e}^{2}}+4)\\pi "] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082905/state.json" "file_sha256" "0c52eab5f19a56af72dfd73c0a6c2de6c87bfe905a74bd6bf942caa8d15febf8"} {"question" "e-9866" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx = t-\\sin t, \\quad y = 1-\\cos t, \\quad t\\in \\left[0,\\pi\\right].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082905/example-9866.json" "file_sha256" "c871c70a89ed4ef878f5edb10a5d20e10a45522899a36da6315ef50c145db5c3"} {"question" "e-9866" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS = \\int 2\\pi y\\,\\textrm d s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm d s = \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi \\int_{t_1}^{t_2} y \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from $t_1=0$ to $t_2=\\pi.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\sqrt{{(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\sqrt{1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the identity $\\sin^2 t +\\cos^2 t= 1,$ we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\sqrt{1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\sqrt{1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\sqrt{2-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\sqrt{2(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082905/example-9866.json" "file_sha256" "c871c70a89ed4ef878f5edb10a5d20e10a45522899a36da6315ef50c145db5c3"} {"question" "e-9867" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx = \\sin t, \\quad y = e^t, \\quad t\\in \\left[0,1\\right].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082905/example-9867.json" "file_sha256" "5ef71d93d06ccbf210ad3a85861043231caca0c861a1947222526e85a55f7802"} {"question" "e-9867" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS = \\int 2\\pi x\\,\\textrm d s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm d s = \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi \\int_{t_1}^{t_2} x \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from $t_1=0$ to $t_2=1.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})={e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{({e}^{t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡t+{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082905/example-9867.json" "file_sha256" "5ef71d93d06ccbf210ad3a85861043231caca0c861a1947222526e85a55f7802"} {"question" "e-9869" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx = 1+3t, \\quad y = 2t^{3/2}, \\quad t\\in \\left[0,1\\right].\n$$\n\nCalculate the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082905/example-9869.json" "file_sha256" "34f28911ba1f8d6299ebdfc0ba03476d0bddbf458da1f254c41ae5cb098398fd"} {"question" "e-9869" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS = \\int 2\\pi x\\,\\textrm d s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm d s = \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi \\int_{t_1}^{t_2} x \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from $t_1=0$ to $t_2=1.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+3t)=3 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3/2})=3{t}^{1/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(1+3t)\\sqrt{{(3)}^{2}+{(3{t}^{1/2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(1+3t)\\sqrt{9+9t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(1+3t)\\sqrt{9(1+t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(1+3t)\\cdot 3\\sqrt{1+t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(1+3t)\\sqrt{1+t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the last integral using the substitution $u = 1+t.$ Then, $1+3t=3u-2$ and $\\textrm{d}u = \\textrm d t.$ We also need to compute new limits, given below.\n\n| **$t$** | $0$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =6\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(1+3t)\\sqrt{1+t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}(3u-2)\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =6\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}(3{u}^{3/2}-2{u}^{1/2})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =6\\pi \\cdot {[\\frac{6{u}^{5/2}}{5}-\\frac{4{u}^{3/2}}{3}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =6\\pi [(\\frac{6({2}^{5/2})}{5}-\\frac{4({2}^{3/2})}{3})-(\\frac{6}{5}-\\frac{4}{3})] \\\\  & =6\\pi [(\\frac{24\\sqrt{2}}{5}-\\frac{8\\sqrt{2}}{3})-(\\frac{6}{5}-\\frac{4}{3})] \\\\  & =6\\pi [\\frac{32\\sqrt{2}}{15}+\\frac{2}{15}] \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{5}(16\\sqrt{2}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082905/example-9869.json" "file_sha256" "34f28911ba1f8d6299ebdfc0ba03476d0bddbf458da1f254c41ae5cb098398fd"}] "usage" {"e-9869" [] "e-9866" [] "e-9867" [] "q-139069" [] "q-138944" [] "q-138981" []} "basis" 2863 "retractions" [[17592186129422 :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$x = \\cos 2t, y = \\cos t, t ∈ [0, \\frac{π}{4}]$.\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2π$ radians about the $x$-axis?"] [17592186129428 :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$x = e^{t}, y = 4t, t ∈ [0, 2]$.\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2π$ radians about the $x$-axis?"] [17592186129426 :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$x = 2\\sin t, y = 2\\cos t, t ∈ [0, π]$.\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2π$ radians about the $y$-axis?"] [17592186129008 :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n$x = t-\\sin t, \\quad y = 1-\\cos t, \\quad t\\in \\left[0,\\pi\\right]$.\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis?"] [17592186129008 :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS = \\int 2\\pi y\\,\\textrm d s\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm d s = \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d t\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi \\int_{t_1}^{t_2} y \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d t\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from $t_1=0$ to $t_2=\\pi$.\n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dx}{dt} &= \\frac{d}{dt}(t - \\sin t) = 1 - \\cos t \\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dt} &= \\frac{d}{dt}(1 - \\cos t) = \\sin t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS &= 2π∫_{t_{1}}^{t_{2}}y\\sqrt{(\\frac{dx}{dt})^{2} + (\\frac{dy}{dt})^{2}}dt \\\\\n&= 2π∫_{0}^{π}(1 - \\cos t)\\sqrt{(1 - \\cos t)^{2} + (\\sin t)^{2}}dt \\\\\n&= 2π∫_{0}^{π}(1 - \\cos t)\\sqrt{1 - 2\\cos t + \\cos^{2} t + \\sin^{2} t}dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the identity\n\n$$\n\\sin^2 t +\\cos^2 t= 1\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS &= 2π∫_{0}^{π}(1 - \\cos t)\\sqrt{1 - 2\\cos t + (\\sin^{2} t + \\cos^{2} t)}dt \\\\\n&= 2π∫_{0}^{π}(1 - \\cos t)\\sqrt{1 - 2\\cos t + 1}dt \\\\\n&= 2π∫_{0}^{π}(1 - \\cos t)\\sqrt{2 - 2\\cos t}dt \\\\\n&= 2π∫_{0}^{π}(1 - \\cos t)\\sqrt{2(1 - \\cos t)}dt \\\\\n&= 2π∫_{0}^{π}(1 - \\cos t) \\cdot \\sqrt{2} \\cdot \\sqrt{1 - \\cos t}dt \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt{2}π∫_{0}^{π}\\sqrt{(1 - \\cos t)^{3}}dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186129009 :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n$x = \\sin t, \\quad y = e^t, \\quad t\\in \\left[0,1\\right]$.\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis?"] [17592186129009 :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS = \\int 2\\pi x\\,\\textrm d s\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm d s = \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d t\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi \\int_{t_1}^{t_2} x \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d t\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from $t_1=0$ to $t_2=1$.\n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dx}{dt} &= \\frac{d}{dt}(\\sin t) = \\cos t \\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dt} &= \\frac{d}{dt}(e^{t}) = e^{t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS &= 2π∫_{t_{1}}^{t_{2}}x\\sqrt{(\\frac{dx}{dt})^{2} + (\\frac{dy}{dt})^{2}}dt \\\\\n&= 2π∫_{0}^{1}\\sin t\\sqrt{(\\cos t)^{2} + (e^{t})^{2}}dt \\\\\n&= 2π∫_{0}^{1}\\sin t\\sqrt{\\cos^{2} t + e^{2t}}dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186129010 :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n$x = 1+3t, \\quad y = 2t^{3/2}, \\quad t\\in \\left[0,1\\right]$.\nCalculate the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis."] [17592186129010 :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS = \\int 2\\pi x\\,\\textrm d s\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm d s = \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d t\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi \\int_{t_1}^{t_2} x \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d t\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from $t_1=0$ to $t_2=1$.\n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dx}{dt} &= \\frac{d}{dt}(1 + 3t) = 3 \\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dt} &= \\frac{d}{dt}(2t^{3/2}) = 3t^{1/2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS &= 2π∫_{t_{1}}^{t_{2}}x\\sqrt{(\\frac{dx}{dt})^{2} + (\\frac{dy}{dt})^{2}}dt \\\\\n&= 2π∫_{0}^{1}(1 + 3t)\\sqrt{(3)^{2} + (3t^{1/2})^{2}}dt \\\\\n&= 2π∫_{0}^{1}(1 + 3t)\\sqrt{9 + 9t}dt \\\\\n&= 2π∫_{0}^{1}(1 + 3t)\\sqrt{9(1 + t)}dt \\\\\n&= 2π∫_{0}^{1}(1 + 3t) \\cdot 3\\sqrt{1 + t}dt \\\\\n&= 6π∫_{0}^{1}(1 + 3t)\\sqrt{1 + t}dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the last integral using the substitution\n\n$$\nu = 1+t\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n$$\n1+3t=3u-2\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\textrm{d}u = \\textrm d t\n$$\n\nWe also need to compute new limits, given below.\n\n| **$t$** | $0$ | $1$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS &= 6π∫_{0}^{1}(1 + 3t)\\sqrt{1 + t}dt \\\\\n&= 6π∫_{1}^{2}(3u - 2)\\sqrt{u}du \\\\\n&= 6π∫_{1}^{2}(3u^{3/2} - 2u^{1/2})du \\\\\n&= 6π \\cdot [\\frac{6u^{5/2}}{5} - \\frac{4u^{3/2}}{3}]_{1}^{2} \\\\\n&= 6π[((6(2^{5/2}))/(5) - (4(2^{3/2}))/(3)) - (\\frac{6}{5} - \\frac{4}{3})] \\\\\n&= 6π[(\\frac{24\\sqrt{2}}{5} - \\frac{8\\sqrt{2}}{3}) - (\\frac{6}{5} - \\frac{4}{3})] \\\\\n&= 6π[\\frac{32\\sqrt{2}}{15} + \\frac{2}{15}] \\\\\n&= \\frac{4π}{5}(16\\sqrt{2} + 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186126339 17592186126340 17592186126341 17592186126342 17592186126343 17592186126363 17592186126364 17592186126365 17592186126366 17592186126367 17592186126375 17592186126376 17592186126377 17592186126378 17592186126379] 